  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1259 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA vigorous and fast-moving shortwave trough will dive out of the \nnorthern Rockies toward the central to southern plains on Saturday. \nConsequently, Saturday's winds will be considerably stronger as the \ntightening gradient aloft causes the northwesterlies to surge up in \nspeed. Latest model projections have increased 700 mb speeds to a \nroughly 30-55 kt range with strongest speeds near and just downwind \nof the central mountain chain, peaking during the afternoon. \nForecast models are converging toward better agreement with the \nplacement and depth of the lee-side surface low over the TX \npanhandle with a stout low level gradient that will help spread \nstrong winds across a large chunk of the forecast area Saturday. \nHighland areas are even flirting with gusts to 60 mph, so a High \nWind Watch might be warranted on subsequent shifts. Temperatures \nwill not undergo any major alterations Saturday with daytime highs \nrising above normal in all areas with the far eastern plains \nobserving the highest departures due to downsloping winds. \nPrecipitation associated with the shortwave will remain minimal in \nNM, staying very light and relegated near and just east of the Raton \nPass through Saturday night. The winds will start to reduce in speed \nin many western and central areas Saturday evening as boundary layer \ndecoupling occurs, but the surface gradient behind the backdoor \nsegment of the front will only intensify, keeping the northeastern \nquadrant of NM windy beyond midnight. \n\nA lighter zonal flow aloft will then be in store on Sunday. Surface \nwinds would veer around, even turning southerly in the plains by the \nafternoon with generally light to moderate speeds. Temperatures will \nbe set back closer to normal in western and central zones while the \nbackdoor segment of the front will have reduced readings 5-10 \ndegrees below average in the plains. \n\nAnother upstream shortwave trough will then cross NM on Monday, \ninducing another period of breezy to windy conditions. Wind speeds \ndo not look quite as strong (generally 5 to 10 mph less) as \nSaturday's given lower speeds aloft and a weaker surface low. The \nshortwave aloft will be another progressive and transient feature \nvoid of moisture, keeping minimal precipitation chances in the \nforecast. Temperatures will remain slightly above normal on Monday, \nreducing closer to average by Tuesday in the wake of Monday's \nshortwave trough. Tuesday's flow aloft would remain brisk, backing a \nbit more southwesterly with breezy to locally windy conditions being \nprobable at the surface.\n\nThe next weather impacts will begin arriving into early Wednesday as \na deeper Pacific low rolls toward NM, carrying a more maritime \nairmass that would support increased precipitation chances.",
